{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-343738" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n\nConsider the curve $x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y=2.$ Given that $x=x(t),\\text{ }y=y(t),$ and that $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=6$ on the curve at the point $(4,\\frac{\\pi }{3}),$ find the value of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}$ at that point. Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/state.json" "file_sha256" "756f34d2959f7b142e8a2525dbfd1187391452cb0a7fa7a77dcc3d3dd3551be5"} {"question" "q-343738" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡y-x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=6,$ $x=4,$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ and solving for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡y-x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =0 \\\\ 6\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-4\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =0 \\\\ 6\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}-4\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =0 \\\\ 3-2\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/state.json" "file_sha256" "756f34d2959f7b142e8a2525dbfd1187391452cb0a7fa7a77dcc3d3dd3551be5"} {"question" "q-343738" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3e5b6657262ce7c7a8a3f22e5ed238b727dd20c7a9ba88395b1496a7779dfef4"} {"question" "q-343738" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/state.json" "file_sha256" "756f34d2959f7b142e8a2525dbfd1187391452cb0a7fa7a77dcc3d3dd3551be5"} {"question" "q-343738" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/state.json" "file_sha256" "756f34d2959f7b142e8a2525dbfd1187391452cb0a7fa7a77dcc3d3dd3551be5"} {"question" "q-156854" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the parabola ${x}^{2}-8y=0.$ Given that $x=x(t),$ $y=y(t),$ and that $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{8}$ on the curve at the point $(4,2),$ find the value of  $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}$ at that point." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/state.json" "file_sha256" "756f34d2959f7b142e8a2525dbfd1187391452cb0a7fa7a77dcc3d3dd3551be5"} {"question" "q-156854" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-8y & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}-8y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(8y) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(8y) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (2x)-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 8 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{8},$ $x=4,$ and $y=2,$ and solve $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (2\\cdot (4))-(\\frac{1}{8})\\cdot 8 & =0 \\\\ 8\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-1 & =0 \\\\ 8\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/state.json" "file_sha256" "756f34d2959f7b142e8a2525dbfd1187391452cb0a7fa7a77dcc3d3dd3551be5"} {"question" "q-156854" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3e5b6657262ce7c7a8a3f22e5ed238b727dd20c7a9ba88395b1496a7779dfef4"} {"question" "q-156854" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/state.json" "file_sha256" "756f34d2959f7b142e8a2525dbfd1187391452cb0a7fa7a77dcc3d3dd3551be5"} {"question" "q-156854" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "3"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{8}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/state.json" "file_sha256" "756f34d2959f7b142e8a2525dbfd1187391452cb0a7fa7a77dcc3d3dd3551be5"} {"question" "q-156854" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{8}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/state.json" "file_sha256" "756f34d2959f7b142e8a2525dbfd1187391452cb0a7fa7a77dcc3d3dd3551be5"} {"question" "q-343745" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n\nConsider the curve $\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+y=0.$ Given that $x=x(t),\\text{ }y=y(t),$ and that $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=5$ on the curve at the point $(1,0),$ find the value of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}$ at that point.\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/state.json" "file_sha256" "756f34d2959f7b142e8a2525dbfd1187391452cb0a7fa7a77dcc3d3dd3551be5"} {"question" "q-343745" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=5,$ $x=1,$ and $y=0,$ and solving for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =0 \\\\ 5\\cdot \\frac{1}{1}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =0 \\\\ 5+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/state.json" "file_sha256" "756f34d2959f7b142e8a2525dbfd1187391452cb0a7fa7a77dcc3d3dd3551be5"} {"question" "q-343745" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3e5b6657262ce7c7a8a3f22e5ed238b727dd20c7a9ba88395b1496a7779dfef4"} {"question" "q-343745" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/state.json" "file_sha256" "756f34d2959f7b142e8a2525dbfd1187391452cb0a7fa7a77dcc3d3dd3551be5"} {"question" "q-343745" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-5"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/state.json" "file_sha256" "756f34d2959f7b142e8a2525dbfd1187391452cb0a7fa7a77dcc3d3dd3551be5"} {"question" "q-156756" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the curve ${e}^{x}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y=1.$ Given that $x=x(t),$ $y=y(t),$ and that $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=3$ on the curve at the point $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ find the value of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}$ at that point." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/state.json" "file_sha256" "756f34d2959f7b142e8a2525dbfd1187391452cb0a7fa7a77dcc3d3dd3551be5"} {"question" "q-156756" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{x}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡y) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡y) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot ({e}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNow, we substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=3,$ $x=0,$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ and solve $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot {e}^{0}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})) & =0 \\\\ 3+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (-1) & =0 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =-3 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/state.json" "file_sha256" "756f34d2959f7b142e8a2525dbfd1187391452cb0a7fa7a77dcc3d3dd3551be5"} {"question" "q-156756" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3e5b6657262ce7c7a8a3f22e5ed238b727dd20c7a9ba88395b1496a7779dfef4"} {"question" "q-156756" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/state.json" "file_sha256" "756f34d2959f7b142e8a2525dbfd1187391452cb0a7fa7a77dcc3d3dd3551be5"} {"question" "q-156756" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2"] ["math" "-3"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "6"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/state.json" "file_sha256" "756f34d2959f7b142e8a2525dbfd1187391452cb0a7fa7a77dcc3d3dd3551be5"} {"question" "q-156756" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/state.json" "file_sha256" "756f34d2959f7b142e8a2525dbfd1187391452cb0a7fa7a77dcc3d3dd3551be5"} {"question" "q-343557" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the parabola ${y}^{2}-20x=0.$ Given that $x=x(t),$ $y=y(t),$ and that $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{3}{10}$ on the curve at the point $(5,10),$ find the value of  $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}$ at that point.\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/state.json" "file_sha256" "756f34d2959f7b142e8a2525dbfd1187391452cb0a7fa7a77dcc3d3dd3551be5"} {"question" "q-343557" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-20x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({y}^{2}-20x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({y}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(20x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(20x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (2y)-\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 20 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{3}{10},$ $x=5,$ and $y=10,$ and solving for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (2y)-\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 20 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (2\\cdot 10)-(\\frac{3}{10})\\cdot 20 & =0 \\\\ 20\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-6 & =0 \\\\ 20\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =6 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{3}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/state.json" "file_sha256" "756f34d2959f7b142e8a2525dbfd1187391452cb0a7fa7a77dcc3d3dd3551be5"} {"question" "q-343557" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3e5b6657262ce7c7a8a3f22e5ed238b727dd20c7a9ba88395b1496a7779dfef4"} {"question" "q-343557" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/state.json" "file_sha256" "756f34d2959f7b142e8a2525dbfd1187391452cb0a7fa7a77dcc3d3dd3551be5"} {"question" "q-343557" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3}{10}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/state.json" "file_sha256" "756f34d2959f7b142e8a2525dbfd1187391452cb0a7fa7a77dcc3d3dd3551be5"} {"question" "q-157100" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the curve ${x}^{3}-4{y}^{2}-2y=2.$ Given that $x=x(t),$   $y=y(t),$  and that $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=2$ on the curve at the point where $y=1,$ find the value of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}$ at that point." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/state.json" "file_sha256" "756f34d2959f7b142e8a2525dbfd1187391452cb0a7fa7a77dcc3d3dd3551be5"} {"question" "q-157100" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-4{y}^{2}-2y & =2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{3}-4{y}^{2}-2y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{3})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{y}^{2}+2y) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3})-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(4{y}^{2}+2y) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (3{x}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (8y+2) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=2$ and $y=1.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (3{x}^{2})-2\\cdot 10 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also want to substitute for $x,$ but we are not given this value. However, we can find it by substituting $y=1$ into the equation of the curve.\n\nAt the point where $y=1,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-4\\cdot {1}^{2}-2\\cdot 1 & =2 \\\\ {x}^{3}-6 & =2 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =8 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 3\\cdot {2}^{2}-20 & =0 \\\\ 12\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =20 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/state.json" "file_sha256" "756f34d2959f7b142e8a2525dbfd1187391452cb0a7fa7a77dcc3d3dd3551be5"} {"question" "q-157100" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3e5b6657262ce7c7a8a3f22e5ed238b727dd20c7a9ba88395b1496a7779dfef4"} {"question" "q-157100" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/state.json" "file_sha256" "756f34d2959f7b142e8a2525dbfd1187391452cb0a7fa7a77dcc3d3dd3551be5"} {"question" "q-157100" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{4}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/state.json" "file_sha256" "756f34d2959f7b142e8a2525dbfd1187391452cb0a7fa7a77dcc3d3dd3551be5"} {"question" "q-157100" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{5}{3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/state.json" "file_sha256" "756f34d2959f7b142e8a2525dbfd1187391452cb0a7fa7a77dcc3d3dd3551be5"} {"question" "q-157155" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the curve $-{y}^{3}+2(x-2)^{2}=10.$ Given that $x=x(t),$   $y=y(t),$  and that $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{4}$ on the curve at the point where $x=3,$ find the value of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}$ at that point." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/state.json" "file_sha256" "756f34d2959f7b142e8a2525dbfd1187391452cb0a7fa7a77dcc3d3dd3551be5"} {"question" "q-157155" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{y}^{3}+2(x-2)^{2} & =10 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-{y}^{3}+2(x-2)^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(10) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-{y}^{3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2(x-2)^{2}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(-{y}^{3})+\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2(x-2)^{2}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (-3{y}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 4(x-2) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{4}$ and $x=3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (-3{y}^{2})+\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 4(3-2) & =0 \\\\ (-3{y}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also want to substitute for $y,$ but we are not given this value. However, we can find it by substituting $x=3$ into the equation of the curve. \n\nAt the point where $x=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{y}^{3}+2(3-2)^{2} & =10 \\\\ -{y}^{3}+2 & =10 \\\\ {y}^{3} & =-8 \\\\ y & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $y=-2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3(-2)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+1 & =0 \\\\ -12\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+1 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/state.json" "file_sha256" "756f34d2959f7b142e8a2525dbfd1187391452cb0a7fa7a77dcc3d3dd3551be5"} {"question" "q-157155" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3e5b6657262ce7c7a8a3f22e5ed238b727dd20c7a9ba88395b1496a7779dfef4"} {"question" "q-157155" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/state.json" "file_sha256" "756f34d2959f7b142e8a2525dbfd1187391452cb0a7fa7a77dcc3d3dd3551be5"} {"question" "q-157155" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "3"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{12}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/state.json" "file_sha256" "756f34d2959f7b142e8a2525dbfd1187391452cb0a7fa7a77dcc3d3dd3551be5"} {"question" "q-157155" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{12}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/state.json" "file_sha256" "756f34d2959f7b142e8a2525dbfd1187391452cb0a7fa7a77dcc3d3dd3551be5"} {"question" "q-115951" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the curve ${e}^{x}-{e}^{y}=y.$ Given that $x=x(t),$   $y=y(t),$  and $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-3$ on the curve at the point where $y=0,$ find the value of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}$ at that point." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/state.json" "file_sha256" "756f34d2959f7b142e8a2525dbfd1187391452cb0a7fa7a77dcc3d3dd3551be5"} {"question" "q-115951" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}-{e}^{y} & =y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{x}-{e}^{y}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{x})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{y}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({e}^{y}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot {e}^{x}-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot {e}^{y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot {e}^{x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot ({e}^{y}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-3$ and $y=0.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-3)\\cdot {e}^{x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot ({e}^{0}+1) \\\\ -3{e}^{x} & =2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also want to substitute for $x,$ but we are not given this value. However, we can find it by substituting $y=0$ into the equation of the curve.\n\nAt the point where $y=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}-{e}^{y} & =y \\\\ {e}^{x}-{e}^{0} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{x}-1 & =0 \\\\ {e}^{x} & =1 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{e}^{0} & =2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -3 & =2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/state.json" "file_sha256" "756f34d2959f7b142e8a2525dbfd1187391452cb0a7fa7a77dcc3d3dd3551be5"} {"question" "q-115951" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3e5b6657262ce7c7a8a3f22e5ed238b727dd20c7a9ba88395b1496a7779dfef4"} {"question" "q-115951" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/state.json" "file_sha256" "756f34d2959f7b142e8a2525dbfd1187391452cb0a7fa7a77dcc3d3dd3551be5"} {"question" "q-115951" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{3}{2}"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{e}"] ["math" "e"] ["math" "{e}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/state.json" "file_sha256" "756f34d2959f7b142e8a2525dbfd1187391452cb0a7fa7a77dcc3d3dd3551be5"} {"question" "q-115951" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{3}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/state.json" "file_sha256" "756f34d2959f7b142e8a2525dbfd1187391452cb0a7fa7a77dcc3d3dd3551be5"} {"question" "q-48800" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the curve $x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y=4.$ Given that $x=x(t),$   $y=y(t),$  and that $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-1$ on the curve at the point where $x=4,$ find the value of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}$ at that point." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/state.json" "file_sha256" "756f34d2959f7b142e8a2525dbfd1187391452cb0a7fa7a77dcc3d3dd3551be5"} {"question" "q-48800" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =4 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 1+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{1}{y} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-1$ and $x=4.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{1}{y} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also want to substitute for $y,$ but we are not given this value. However, we can find it by substituting $x=4$ into the equation of the curve.\n\nAt the point where $x=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =4 \\\\ 4+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =4 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =0 \\\\ y & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $y=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 1 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/state.json" "file_sha256" "756f34d2959f7b142e8a2525dbfd1187391452cb0a7fa7a77dcc3d3dd3551be5"} {"question" "q-48800" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3e5b6657262ce7c7a8a3f22e5ed238b727dd20c7a9ba88395b1496a7779dfef4"} {"question" "q-48800" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/state.json" "file_sha256" "756f34d2959f7b142e8a2525dbfd1187391452cb0a7fa7a77dcc3d3dd3551be5"} {"question" "q-48800" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/state.json" "file_sha256" "756f34d2959f7b142e8a2525dbfd1187391452cb0a7fa7a77dcc3d3dd3551be5"} {"question" "q-48800" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/state.json" "file_sha256" "756f34d2959f7b142e8a2525dbfd1187391452cb0a7fa7a77dcc3d3dd3551be5"} {"question" "q-315191" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the curve ${y}^{2}+2y=-x.$ Given that $x=x(t),$ $y=y(t),$ and that $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=2$ on the curve at the point where $x=1,$ find the value of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}$ at that point.\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=$ \n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/state.json" "file_sha256" "756f34d2959f7b142e8a2525dbfd1187391452cb0a7fa7a77dcc3d3dd3551be5"} {"question" "q-315191" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+2y & =-x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({y}^{2}+2y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{2}+2y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (2y+2) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (-1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (2y+2) & =-\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=2$ and $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(2y+2) & =-\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also want to substitute for $y,$ but we are not given this value. However, we can find it by substituting $x=1$ into the equation of the curve.\n\nAt the point where $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+2y & =-x \\\\ {y}^{2}+2y & =-1 \\\\ {y}^{2}+2y+1 & =0 \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ y & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $y=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(2y+2) & =-\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(2\\cdot (-1)+2) & =-\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(0) & =-\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 0 & =-\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/state.json" "file_sha256" "756f34d2959f7b142e8a2525dbfd1187391452cb0a7fa7a77dcc3d3dd3551be5"} {"question" "q-315191" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3e5b6657262ce7c7a8a3f22e5ed238b727dd20c7a9ba88395b1496a7779dfef4"} {"question" "q-315191" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/state.json" "file_sha256" "756f34d2959f7b142e8a2525dbfd1187391452cb0a7fa7a77dcc3d3dd3551be5"} {"question" "q-315191" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/state.json" "file_sha256" "756f34d2959f7b142e8a2525dbfd1187391452cb0a7fa7a77dcc3d3dd3551be5"} {"question" "q-344033" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the section of the ellipse $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1$ that lies in the first quadrant.  Given that $x=x(t),$ $y=y(t),$  and that $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=3$ on the ellipse at the point where $y=3,$ find the value of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}$ at that point.\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/state.json" "file_sha256" "756f34d2959f7b142e8a2525dbfd1187391452cb0a7fa7a77dcc3d3dd3551be5"} {"question" "q-344033" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16})+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (\\frac{2x}{16})+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (\\frac{2y}{25}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, substituting the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=3$ and $y=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (\\frac{2x}{16})+3\\cdot (\\frac{2(3)}{25}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{x}{8}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{18}{25} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also want to substitute for $x,$ but we are not given this value. However, we can find it by substituting $y=3$ into the equation of the ellipse.  \n\nAt the point where $y=3,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{3}^{2}}{25} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{9}{25} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{16} & =\\frac{16}{25} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{256}{25} \\\\ x & =\\frac{16}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used $x>0$ since we're only interested in the first quadrant. \n\nFinally, substituting $x=\\frac{16}{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{8}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{18}{25} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{8}(\\frac{16}{5})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{18}{25} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{18}{25} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{18}{25}\\cdot \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{9}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/state.json" "file_sha256" "756f34d2959f7b142e8a2525dbfd1187391452cb0a7fa7a77dcc3d3dd3551be5"} {"question" "q-344033" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3e5b6657262ce7c7a8a3f22e5ed238b727dd20c7a9ba88395b1496a7779dfef4"} {"question" "q-344033" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/state.json" "file_sha256" "756f34d2959f7b142e8a2525dbfd1187391452cb0a7fa7a77dcc3d3dd3551be5"} {"question" "q-344033" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{9}{5}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/state.json" "file_sha256" "756f34d2959f7b142e8a2525dbfd1187391452cb0a7fa7a77dcc3d3dd3551be5"} {"question" "q-313938" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the section of the hyperbola $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1$ that lies in the second quadrant. Given that $x=x(t),$ $y=y(t),$ and that $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=12$ on the hyperbola at the point where $x=-5,$ find the value of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}$ at that point.\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/state.json" "file_sha256" "756f34d2959f7b142e8a2525dbfd1187391452cb0a7fa7a77dcc3d3dd3551be5"} {"question" "q-313938" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9})-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (\\frac{2x}{9})-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (\\frac{2y}{9}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=12$ and $x=-5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12\\cdot (\\frac{2(-5)}{9})-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (\\frac{2y}{9}) & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{120}{9}-\\frac{2y}{9}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also want to substitute for $y,$ but we are not given this value. However, we can find it by substituting $x=-5$ into the equation of the hyperbola.\n\nAt the point where $x=-5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(-5)^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{25}{9}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ 25-{y}^{2} & =9 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =16 \\\\ y & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used $y>0$ since we're only interested in the second quadrant.\n\nFinally, substituting $y=4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{120}{9}-\\frac{2(4)}{9}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{120}{9}-\\frac{8}{9}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{8}{9}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{120}{9} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{120}{8} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =-15.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/state.json" "file_sha256" "756f34d2959f7b142e8a2525dbfd1187391452cb0a7fa7a77dcc3d3dd3551be5"} {"question" "q-313938" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3e5b6657262ce7c7a8a3f22e5ed238b727dd20c7a9ba88395b1496a7779dfef4"} {"question" "q-313938" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/state.json" "file_sha256" "756f34d2959f7b142e8a2525dbfd1187391452cb0a7fa7a77dcc3d3dd3551be5"} {"question" "q-313938" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-15"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/state.json" "file_sha256" "756f34d2959f7b142e8a2525dbfd1187391452cb0a7fa7a77dcc3d3dd3551be5"} {"question" "q-157223" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the section of the parabola ${x}^{2}-4y=4$ that lies in the first quadrant.  Given that $x=x(t),$ $y=y(t),$  and that $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=3$ on the parabola at the point where $y=3,$ find the value of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}$ at that point." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/state.json" "file_sha256" "756f34d2959f7b142e8a2525dbfd1187391452cb0a7fa7a77dcc3d3dd3551be5"} {"question" "q-157223" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-4y & =4 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}-4y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4y) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(4y) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (2x)-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 4 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=3$ and $y=3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (2x)-(3)\\cdot 4 & =0 \\\\ 2x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-12 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also want to substitute for $x,$ but we are not given this value. However, we can find it by substituting $y=3$ into the equation of  the parabola.  \n\nAt the point where $y=3,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-4y & =4 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4\\cdot (3) & =4 \\\\ {x}^{2}-12 & =4 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =16 \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used $x>0$ since we're only interested in the  first  quadrant. \n\nFinally, substituting $x=4,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-12 & =0 \\\\ 8\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =12 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{12}{8} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/state.json" "file_sha256" "756f34d2959f7b142e8a2525dbfd1187391452cb0a7fa7a77dcc3d3dd3551be5"} {"question" "q-157223" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3e5b6657262ce7c7a8a3f22e5ed238b727dd20c7a9ba88395b1496a7779dfef4"} {"question" "q-157223" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/state.json" "file_sha256" "756f34d2959f7b142e8a2525dbfd1187391452cb0a7fa7a77dcc3d3dd3551be5"} {"question" "q-157223" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/state.json" "file_sha256" "756f34d2959f7b142e8a2525dbfd1187391452cb0a7fa7a77dcc3d3dd3551be5"} {"question" "q-157223" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/state.json" "file_sha256" "756f34d2959f7b142e8a2525dbfd1187391452cb0a7fa7a77dcc3d3dd3551be5"} {"question" "q-157156" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the section of the hyperbola $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}-{y}^{2}=1$ that lies in the first quadrant. Given that $x=x(t),$ $y=y(t),$ and that $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=6$ on the curve at the point where $x=2\\sqrt{3},$ find the value of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}$ at that point." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/state.json" "file_sha256" "756f34d2959f7b142e8a2525dbfd1187391452cb0a7fa7a77dcc3d3dd3551be5"} {"question" "q-157156" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}-{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}-{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3})-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (\\frac{2x}{3})-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (2y) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=6$ and $x=2\\sqrt{3}.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\cdot (\\frac{2\\cdot 2\\sqrt{3}}{3})-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (2y) & =0 \\\\ 8\\sqrt{3}-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe also want to substitute for $y,$ but we are not given this value. However, we can find it by substituting $x=2\\sqrt{3}$ into the equation of the hyperbola.   \n\nAt the point where $x=2\\sqrt{3},$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}-{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(2\\sqrt{3})^{2}}{3}-{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ 4-{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =3 \\\\ y & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used $y>0$ since we're only interested in the first quadrant. \n\nFinally, substituting $y=\\sqrt{3},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\sqrt{3}-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2(\\sqrt{3}) & =0 \\\\ 8\\sqrt{3}-2\\sqrt{3}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/state.json" "file_sha256" "756f34d2959f7b142e8a2525dbfd1187391452cb0a7fa7a77dcc3d3dd3551be5"} {"question" "q-157156" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3e5b6657262ce7c7a8a3f22e5ed238b727dd20c7a9ba88395b1496a7779dfef4"} {"question" "q-157156" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/state.json" "file_sha256" "756f34d2959f7b142e8a2525dbfd1187391452cb0a7fa7a77dcc3d3dd3551be5"} {"question" "q-157156" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "3"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/state.json" "file_sha256" "756f34d2959f7b142e8a2525dbfd1187391452cb0a7fa7a77dcc3d3dd3551be5"} {"question" "q-157156" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/state.json" "file_sha256" "756f34d2959f7b142e8a2525dbfd1187391452cb0a7fa7a77dcc3d3dd3551be5"} {"question" "q-157217" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the section of the parabola $3{x}^{2}+4y=1$ that lies in the fourth quadrant.  Given that $x=x(t),$ $y=y(t),$  and that $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{1}{4}$ on the parabola at the point where $y=-\\frac{1}{2},$ find the value of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}$ at that point." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/state.json" "file_sha256" "756f34d2959f7b142e8a2525dbfd1187391452cb0a7fa7a77dcc3d3dd3551be5"} {"question" "q-157217" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+4y & =1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{x}^{2}+4y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4y) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(4y) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (6x)+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 4 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{1}{4}$ and $y=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (6x)+(-\\frac{1}{4})\\cdot (4) & =0 \\\\ 6x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also want to substitute for $x,$ but we are not given this value. However, we can find it by substituting $y=-\\frac{1}{2}$ into the equation of  the parabola.  \n\nAt the point where $y=-\\frac{1}{2},$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+4y & =1 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+4\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) & =1 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-2 & =1 \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & =3 \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & =3 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used $x>0$ since we're only interested in the fourth quadrant.  \n\nFinally, substituting $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\cdot (1)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-1 & =0 \\\\ 6\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-1 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/state.json" "file_sha256" "756f34d2959f7b142e8a2525dbfd1187391452cb0a7fa7a77dcc3d3dd3551be5"} {"question" "q-157217" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3e5b6657262ce7c7a8a3f22e5ed238b727dd20c7a9ba88395b1496a7779dfef4"} {"question" "q-157217" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/state.json" "file_sha256" "756f34d2959f7b142e8a2525dbfd1187391452cb0a7fa7a77dcc3d3dd3551be5"} {"question" "q-157217" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-4"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{6}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/state.json" "file_sha256" "756f34d2959f7b142e8a2525dbfd1187391452cb0a7fa7a77dcc3d3dd3551be5"} {"question" "q-157217" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{6}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/state.json" "file_sha256" "756f34d2959f7b142e8a2525dbfd1187391452cb0a7fa7a77dcc3d3dd3551be5"} {"question" "e-10912" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following ellipse: \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{4}=1\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven that $x=x(t),$ $y=y(t),$ and that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}=\\sqrt{2}$ on the curve at the point $(1,\\sqrt2),$ find the value of $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}$ at that point." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/example-10912.json" "file_sha256" "a8a7d518a371ddfe48507eb3998da7880c072801d5c43b97c6e02b935cef2dcc"} {"question" "e-10912" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (x)+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (\\frac{y}{2}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given information $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}=\\sqrt{2},$ $x=1,$ and $y=\\sqrt2,$ and solve $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}.$  We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (1)+\\sqrt{2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+1 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/example-10912.json" "file_sha256" "a8a7d518a371ddfe48507eb3998da7880c072801d5c43b97c6e02b935cef2dcc"} {"question" "e-10900" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the curve $x^3-\\ln y=1.$ Given that $x=x(t),$   $y=y(t),$  and that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}=2$ on the curve at the point where $x=1,$ find the value of $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}$ at that point." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/example-10900.json" "file_sha256" "78ff08d361026b58bcf692c88299aef6505c69f26a2f3ab8543958ccb3fa75e2"} {"question" "e-10900" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{3}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{3})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3})-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 3{x}^{2}-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{1}{y} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given information $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}=2$ and $x=1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{1}{y} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also want to substitute for $y,$ but we are not given this value. However, we can find it by substituting $x=1$ into the equation of the curve.\n\nAt the point where $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =1 \\\\ 1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =0 \\\\ y & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $y=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{1}{1} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/example-10900.json" "file_sha256" "78ff08d361026b58bcf692c88299aef6505c69f26a2f3ab8543958ccb3fa75e2"} {"question" "e-10963" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the section of the hyperbola \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{3}-\\dfrac{y^2}{2}=1\n$$\n\nthat lies in the first quadrant. Given that $x=x(t),$ $y=y(t),$ and that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}=-2$ on the hyperbola at the point where $x=3,$ find the value of $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}$ at this point." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/example-10963.json" "file_sha256" "77b93b5815001bf9834774ec25a7214aa7f59f0123a2849d6e28c3300ae0f269"} {"question" "e-10963" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3})-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (\\frac{2x}{3})-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we substitute the given information $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}=-2$ and $x=3.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (\\frac{2\\cdot 3}{3})-(-2)\\cdot y & =0 \\\\ 2\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe also want to substitute for $y$, but we are not given this value. However, we can find it by substituting $x=3$ into the equation of the hyperbola.   \n\nAt the point where $x=3,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{3}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & =2 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ y & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used $y > 0$ since we're only interested in the first quadrant. \n\nFinally, substituting $y= 2,$ we get  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065776/example-10963.json" "file_sha256" "77b93b5815001bf9834774ec25a7214aa7f59f0123a2849d6e28c3300ae0f269"}] "usage" {"e-10900" [] "e-10912" [] "e-10963" [] "q-48800" []} "basis" 2159 "retractions" [[17592186193162 :question/problem "Consider the curve $x + \\ln y = 4$. Given that $x = x(t)$, $y = y(t)$, and that $\\frac{dx}{dt} =-1$ on the curve at the point where $x = 4$, find the value of $\\frac{dy}{dt}$ at that point."] [17592186192312 :question/problem "Consider the following ellipse:\n\n$\\dfrac{x^2}{2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{4}=1$\n\nGiven that $x=x(t)$, $y=y(t)$, and that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}=\\sqrt{2}$ on the curve at the point $(1,\\sqrt2)$, find the value of $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}$ at that point."] [17592186192312 :question/worked-solution "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x^{2}}{2} + \\frac{y^{2}}{4} &= 1 \\\\\n\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{x^{2}}{2} + \\frac{y^{2}}{4}) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(1) \\\\\n\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{x^{2}}{2}) + \\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{y^{2}}{4}) &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{dx}{dt} \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{x^{2}}{2}) + \\frac{dy}{dt} \\cdot \\frac{d}{dy}(\\frac{y^{2}}{4}) &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{dx}{dt} \\cdot (x) + \\frac{dy}{dt} \\cdot (\\frac{y}{2}) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given information\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}=\\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\n$x=1$, and\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt2\n$$\n\nand solve\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nWe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dx}{dt} \\cdot (1) + \\sqrt{2} \\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{dx}{dt} + 1 &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{dx}{dt} &= -1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186192311 :question/problem "Consider the curve $x^3-\\ln y=1$. Given that $x=x(t)$, $y=y(t)$, and that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}=2$ on the curve at the point where $x=1$, find the value of $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}$ at that point."] [17592186192311 :question/worked-solution "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{3} - \\ln y &= 1 \\\\\n\\frac{d}{dt}(x^{3} - \\ln y) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(1) \\\\\n\\frac{d}{dt}(x^{3}) - \\frac{d}{dt}(\\ln y) &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{dx}{dt} \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{3}) - \\frac{dy}{dt} \\cdot \\frac{d}{dy}(\\ln y) &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{dx}{dt} \\cdot 3x^{2} - \\frac{dy}{dt} \\cdot \\frac{1}{y} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given information\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}=2\n$$\n\nand $x=1$. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6 - \\frac{dy}{dt} \\cdot \\frac{1}{y} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also want to substitute for $y$, but we are not given this value. However, we can find it by substituting $x=1$ into the equation of the curve.\n\nAt the point where $x=1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{3} - \\ln y &= 1 \\\\\n1 - \\ln y &= 1 \\\\\n\\ln y &= 0 \\\\\ny &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $y=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6 - \\frac{dy}{dt} \\cdot \\frac{1}{1} &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dt} &= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186192325 :question/problem "Consider the section of the hyperbola\n$\\dfrac{x^2}{3}-\\dfrac{y^2}{2}=1$\nthat lies in the first quadrant. Given that $x=x(t)$, $y=y(t)$, and that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}=-2$ on the hyperbola at the point where $x=3$, find the value of $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}$ at this point."] [17592186192325 :question/worked-solution "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x^{2}}{3} - \\frac{y^{2}}{2} &= 1 \\\\\n\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{x^{2}}{3} - \\frac{y^{2}}{2}) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(1) \\\\\n\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{x^{2}}{3}) - \\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{y^{2}}{2}) &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{dx}{dt} \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{x^{2}}{3}) - \\frac{dy}{dt} \\cdot \\frac{d}{dy}(\\frac{y^{2}}{2}) &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{dx}{dt} \\cdot (\\frac{2x}{3}) - \\frac{dy}{dt} \\cdot y &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given information\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}=-2\n$$\n\nand $x=3$. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dx}{dt} \\cdot (\\frac{2 \\cdot 3}{3}) - (-2) \\cdot y &= 0 \\\\\n2\\frac{dx}{dt} + 2y &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{dx}{dt} &= -y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also want to substitute for $y$, but we are not given this value. However, we can find it by substituting $x=3$ into the equation of the hyperbola.\n\nAt the point where $x=3$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x^{2}}{3} - \\frac{y^{2}}{2} &= 1 \\\\\n\\frac{3^{2}}{3} - \\frac{y^{2}}{2} &= 1 \\\\\n\\frac{y^{2}}{2} &= 2 \\\\\ny^{2} &= 4 \\\\\ny &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used $y > 0$ since we're only interested in the first quadrant.\n\nFinally, substituting $y= 2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dx}{dt} &= -y \\\\\n\\frac{dx}{dt} &= -2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186191761 17592186191762 17592186191763 17592186191764 17592186191765] "immutable_fields" [[17592186191760 :answer-field/correct] [17592186191760 :answer-field/id] [17592186191760 :answer-field/key] [17592186191760 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
